A||N||12||Stan86SE||Experienced Fiero Owners:  Is This A Stupid Idea?||||5||
Z||000000||Stan86SE||05-14-2001||04:17 PM||||I just put a new fuel pump in this past weekend and the ol' daily driver 2.8 Auto SE is running absolutely great again! (Yes!)  After owning this car for 5 years, I've just recently also replaced the EGR valve and tube, EGR solenoid, vacuum lines, heater core, dist cap and rotor, ignition module, pickup coil, ignition coil, and plugs and wires.  Also, new coolant/heater hoses all 'round...<P>I've got a "little" extra cash at the moment, and since I'm pushing 102,000 miles, I'm also kinda thinking about doing some "pre-emptive parts replacement" - specifically, maybe replacing the water pump, along with maybe the timing set, maybe also the alternator.  These are parts that are currently working fine, but my thinking is that, due to the higher mileage, I might be better off to go ahead and replace some of these parts now while I can afford it, instead of at a later tme when I can't and/or find myself stranded.  (A buddy of mine and I do all the labor, btw.)<P>Is this a stupid way to be thinking?  Or, should I go by the "If it ain't broke, don't fix it" rule?  What do some of you more experienced Fiero owners think?  I'd appreciate having your $.02 on this...<P>Thanks in advance!<P>- Stan  <P><p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||04:35 PM||||I think your biggest risk with this approach is that you will spend a lot of time and some money replacing hard to get at stuff like the water pump... and then your engine will throw a rod or blow a seal, and youll need to pull it<P>  and if you had waited to do the same work with the engine pulled it would be a lot easier.<P>  Id stick the cash in a jar and write 'Fiero bumper to bumper warrenty program' on it with a magic marker. Then if something fails, your automatically covered.<P>  The other con is you might break something, like a stud or bolt in the engine block, fixing something that isnt broken, and then your car will be broken.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||05-14-2001||06:02 PM||||theres pros and cons to it.<P>save your money until something really breaks down, like your trans $$$$$  or spend it on things that you really have to change. brakes hoses, belts mostly what you done already.<P>The pro is, like i use to joke around with another guy is<BR>... Soon your fiero is going to be brand new after you replace a couple more parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-14-2001||10:52 PM||||Outside of a catastrophic failure (act of God for taking him two out of three in light-to-light action), most failures will give you warning. <P>The water pumps are pretty obvious. The timing chain starts to sound like rocks are rolling around in the engine when it's cold. 102K miles is way too early for a timing chain to die. <P>Listen often and save your money.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-15-2001||10:57 AM||||READ....IF IT AINT BROKE , DONT FIX IT !!<BR>Preventative maint dont include things like just replacing water pump because its old. Ive had water pumps work fine for over 250,000 miles. Just save the money for when something totally unexpected hits you.(itll also draw interest=more free money)||||reg||1
Z||000005||N_Man||05-15-2001||12:49 PM||||Dont do it.<BR>Waste of time and money.<P>my personal story time now.  When i had my cradle dropped i figured well a new water pump cant hurt, its cheap and easy to get at.  Well no less than 6 months later my brand new water pump is dripping, and to replace it in the car took me an afternoon.<P>Now i know that it was a fluke to get a bad pump off the shelf but it happens.<P>So save yourself the time, money, and greif.  If it aint broke dont fix it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT GT 87||05-15-2001||06:48 PM|||| Don't-----------Buy cool wheels & tires or headers .  Thank the lord  you are so lucky ...I have a rod knock......Arguhhh!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT GT 87||05-15-2001||06:48 PM|||| Don't-----------Buy cool wheels & tires or headers .  Thank the lord  you are so lucky ...I have a rod knock......Arguhhh!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Stan86SE||05-16-2001||01:51 AM||||Thanks for the replies and opinions, everybody!  I agree with what you've said and now think that I'll just leave well enough alone.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dan87GT||05-16-2001||07:07 AM||||With 102K you might want to think about changing the rod bearings and replacing the oil pump. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Greg Piet||05-16-2001||07:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>READ....IF IT AINT BROKE , DONT FIX IT !!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B> LISTEN TO THIS!!!!!!! </B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||RacinRob||05-16-2001||10:37 AM||||is 102000 MI that bad? I have 164000 on the engine and 132000 on the rest of the car.  It has a bad O2 sensor, it also has a really strange knocking (metal to metal clanking) that doesn't change with RPM. (I think something is loose and it has nothing to do with the engine)Other than those two things it runs great.  I think too many people here think that 100000 mi is a magic number that things start breaking, and in my experience it's just not true.  I also beat the p*ss out of mine.   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||10:42 AM||||crawl around under your car and bang on and wiggle the heat sheilds and the exhaust. You might just have a loose piece of sheet metal under there somewhere. coat hanger time!||||reg||1
